transformation-to-cm-frame.interpretation:
The formula 
\mathbf{r}_i \to \mathbf{R}_{\text{cm}}
 represents a conceptual transformation of coordinates rather than a direct calculation for a specific particle.  Here, \(\mathbf{r}_i\) denotes the position vector of the \(i\)-th particle in an inertial frame (often called the lab frame).  The transformation implies that we are now considering the motion of the system from the perspective of a frame whose origin is at the center of mass of the system. The center of mass itself is defined by the vector 
\mathbf{R}_{\text{cm}} = \frac{\sum_i m_i \mathbf{r}_i}{\sum_i m_i}
, where \(m_i\) is the mass of the \(i\)-th particle. When we transform to the CM frame, the position vector of the \(i\)-th particle in this new frame, let's denote it as \(\mathbf{r}'_i\), is related to its position in the lab frame by \(\mathbf{r}'$_i = \mathbf${r}$_i - \mathbf${R}_{\text{cm}}\).  The equation \(\mathbf{r}_i \to \mathbf{R}_{\text{cm}}
 is a shorthand notation indicating that the new reference point for all particle positions \(\mathbf{r}_i\) becomes the location of the center of mass itself. In this frame, the total momentum \(\sum_i m_i \mathbf{v}'_i\) is zero, where \(\mathbf{v}'_i\) is the velocity of the \(i\)-th particle in the CM frame.

transformation-to-cm-frame.limits_and_boundary:
As the number of particles \(N\) in the system approaches infinity, the concept of the center of mass becomes crucial for defining macroscopic properties. In the limit of a continuous mass distribution, the summation in the definition of \(\mathbf{R}_{\text{cm}}
 is replaced by an integral.  If the system's total mass \($M = \sum_i$ m_i\) is zero (which is not physically possible for a system with mass), the concept of a center of mass frame is ill-defined. If the total mass is very large, and the distribution of mass is such that \(\mathbf{R}_{\text{cm}}
 is effectively at the origin of the lab frame, then the lab frame and the CM frame are approximately the same.
